WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.887
Top K controls how many chunks retrieval returns.

00:00:03.887 --> 00:00:09.552
It is tempting to let the model choose any number, but this is often unnecessary.

00:00:09.552 --> 00:00:15.167
A tested application default such as three or four chunks may be more reliable.

00:00:15.662 --> 00:00:19.764
If K is too small, you may miss needed evidence.

00:00:19.764 --> 00:00:27.559
If it is too large, you may add irrelevant text, increase context size, increase latency, and make grading harder.

00:00:27.559 --> 00:00:32.534
The right value should come from evaluation.

00:00:32.534 --> 00:00:36.224
For some projects, three chunks may work well.

00:00:36.224 --> 00:00:39.622
For others, you may need five or a reranker.

00:00:40.097 --> 00:00:45.039
If you decide to expose top K to the agent, keep the range narrow.

00:00:45.039 --> 00:00:51.779
For example, allow values from one to eight instead of allowing fifty or one hundred.

00:00:51.779 --> 00:00:58.550
Retrieval parameters are application policy, not automatically something the agent should control.
